A||||4||JohnnyK||Tach won't work||||5||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||03-21-2000||02:09 PM||||I'm having a problem here with a tach that doesn't work (Hasn't since I got the car (that wasn't too long ago)).  A bunch of people told me that the tach filter might not be hooked up, but it looks as if everything is.. someone have a picture of it or something? Thanks<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||03-21-2000||02:51 PM||||If the other gauges are working, it's likely the tach filter. Where it is depends on engine...<P>In any event, it looks like an old-fashioned ignition condenser with two wires coming out of it...it may be fastened to a bolt near the thermostat housing on the 4 and near the coil on the 6.<P>Maybe someone else can be more specific as to location on the 6.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||03-21-2000||03:07 PM||||On the v-6 it is either under the coil or below the dist. cap. Even if the filter looks good, doesn't mean it is. Rodney Dickman sell them. <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodney||03-21-2000||03:22 PM||||The way to test if the tach filter is bad is to disconnect it from the engine wiring harness at the connector (2 white wires) and put a jumper across the engine harness plug wire ends. Don't let the jumper ground out. If the tach works the filter is bad. Do this for a test only as the voltage to the tach is much higher when jumpered out. <P>This is the test for when the tach does not work or works on and off. A jumpy tach is usually related to bad wires, spark plugs and/or pick up coil etc.. Some times a new tach filter will correct these symptoms but it is not the best way to fix a jumpy tach.<P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||03-21-2000||11:39 PM||||Well, connecting the two wires worked.. I think I'm off to the junkyard tommorow to snag one.. (What happens if I leave them connected without the filter? Thanks)<BR>||||reg||1
